insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('the-paperwork-after-someone-dies', 'The Paperwork After Someone Dies', 'Accounts to close, forms that ask the same question nine times, and hold music while you are barely upright. How to get through the administration of a death.', 'You are on hold to a utility company, explaining for the fourth time this week that your father has died. The person is kind. The system is not. You have a folder on the kitchen table that is somehow both empty and overwhelming.

Nobody warns you that grief comes with a job attached, and that the job arrives at the exact moment you are least able to do anything.

Why this is harder than it looks on paper

The tasks themselves are mostly ordinary: forms, phone calls, certificates. What makes them brutal is everything around them.

You are doing administration while in shock, which is a genuine state rather than a figure of speech: concentration goes, short-term memory goes, and simple decisions take far longer than usual.

Every call means saying the sentence again. Each one is a small reopening, and by the eighth you are either numb or in tears on hold, and both feel wrong.

And there is a false urgency in the air. It feels as though everything must be done immediately. Very little of it must.

Sort it into three piles

Before doing anything, split the list, because treating it all as urgent is what flattens people.

Now: registering the death, the funeral arrangements, and anything to do with a tenancy or pets or a house standing empty. Those have real deadlines.

Soon: notifying banks, pensions, benefits, insurance, employer, landlord or mortgage lender, utilities, and starting probate if there is any. Weeks, not days.

Later: closing social media accounts, subscriptions, the car, the clothes, the house itself. Months, and often longer.

Write the three lists on paper and keep them in one place. The folder on the table is doing more work than you think; the point is that nothing has to live in your head.

Get more copies than you think you need

Almost every organization wants proof, and photocopies are frequently refused.

So order several certified copies of the death certificate when you register the death. Four or five saves many weeks of posting one document around the country.

Ask each organization the same two questions: what exactly do you need from me, and is there a bereavement team I should be speaking to? Most large companies have one, they are far better trained than the general line, and almost nobody knows to ask.

Make the calls easier on yourself

Batch them, two or three at a time, in the morning, with tea and a pen, not at night when you are tired.

Have a script and read it: I am calling because my mother died on the twelfth, I am her executor, I need to know what you require. Reading it rather than improvising takes a surprising amount out of it.

Keep one notebook with the date, who you spoke to, the reference number, and what happens next. Everything will go round again at least once, and the notebook is what stops you repeating the whole process from memory.

And let someone else do some of them. A friend, a sibling, an adult child can make a list of calls with your authority, and people who want to help are usually relieved to be given something concrete.

Get help with the parts that are genuinely technical

Probate, inheritance tax, pensions and anything involving a business or a property abroad are not common sense, and guessing is expensive.

A solicitor or an accountant for a few hours is often cheaper than a mistake, and in many cases the estate pays. If money is tight, free advice exists: bereavement charities, legal aid clinics, the probate office''s own helpline, and your own bank''s bereavement team.

If there is no money in the estate, say so out loud to each creditor. Debts do not pass to relatives in most circumstances, and nobody should be paying a dead person''s credit card out of their own wages without advice.

Do not decide the big things yet

In the middle of all this you will be asked, often by well-meaning family, what you are going to do about the house, the money, the car, the move.

Where you can, answer: not yet. Decisions made in the first months are made by someone who is not at full capacity, and almost nothing is improved by rushing.

The exceptions are real deadlines and anything losing money. Those are worth doing on a timetable somebody else helps you set.

Expect the admin to carry feelings in it

You will be fine for an hour and then undone by the smallest thing: their handwriting on a direct debit form, their voice still on the answering machine at the bank, a letter arriving addressed to them in November.

That is normal and it is not a sign that you are handling this badly. Stop when it happens. Nobody has ever been harmed by a form being posted tomorrow.

And set a limit: one or two tasks a day, then nothing. This work expands to fill whatever you give it, and it will happily take months of your evenings.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if you are not sleeping, not eating, unable to work, or finding that weeks are passing with no change at all in how heavy it is.

Bereavement services will often see you without a referral, and they are used to people who are three forms into probate and cannot stop crying on the phone; so are the bereavement teams at banks and utilities, more than you would expect. Grief counseling is a reasonable thing to ask for while the administration is still going on, not only afterward.

And if it has taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Loss', 'Resilience']::text[], 0, '2026-10-16T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-your-child-has-a-panic-attack', 'When Your Child Has a Panic Attack', 'They cannot breathe, their heart is racing, and they are frightened they are dying. What to do in the moment with a child in panic, and what helps afterward.', 'Your daughter is on the bathroom floor saying she cannot breathe. Her hands are tingling, her heart is going, and she is convinced something is seriously wrong with her.

You are frightened too, and the two of you are feeding each other. The first job is getting through the next ten minutes.

What is happening to them

Panic is the body''s alarm going off at full volume with nothing to fight. Adrenaline, a fast heart, quick shallow breathing, tingling hands and lips, chest tightness, feeling unreal or far away, sometimes feeling sick.

All of that is frightening and none of it is dangerous. Panic peaks within a few minutes and comes down on its own; nobody stops breathing from it, and children do not have heart attacks from it.

The tingling and the breathlessness usually come from breathing too fast rather than from not getting enough air, which is why trying to take big gulps makes it worse.

What to do in the moment

Your calm is the main tool you have, and children borrow a parent''s nervous system more than they borrow their words.

So slow everything down. Lower your voice, slow your own breathing, sit down rather than standing over them. Say little and say it in short sentences.

Name it plainly: this is panic, it is horrible, it will pass, I am here. Children generally find a name far more reassuring than reassurance.

Then help the breathing out rather than in. Breathe out slowly with them, longer than the in-breath; blowing through pursed lips, or at an imaginary candle, works better with children than any counting instruction.

Give them something physical to hold on to. Feet flat on the floor, a hand pressed into yours, a cold washcloth, a cold drink, their back against the wall. Something to look at and name out loud: five things you can see in this room.

And do not insist they leave the situation if they can stay. Staying put while it passes teaches them something that leaving does not.

What not to do

Do not ask a lot of questions mid-panic. They cannot think, and questions raise the pressure.

Do not say there is nothing to worry about. The sensations are real, and being told they are imaginary makes a child feel both frightened and unbelieved.

Do not take over the breathing with a complicated technique, and do not use a paper bag, which is not safe advice.

And try not to let your own fear run the room: no rushing, no multiple adults, no immediate talk of hospitals unless something genuinely suggests a physical emergency.

Afterward, keep it ordinary

When it has passed, children take their cue from how you treat it.

Something steady helps: that was panic, you got through it, it went away on its own like it always does. Then a drink, something to eat, something dull and normal.

Avoid the long debrief that evening. Picking over every sensation tends to install the fear more firmly. A short, warm conversation the next day is more useful than an hour at bedtime.

And let them get back to what they were doing where possible. Panic shrinks a child''s world fast, and the thing that keeps it small is the day off, the collection from school, the dropped club.

The pattern matters more than the episode

One panic attack is a bad hour. The worry about the next one is what turns it into a problem.

So watch for avoidance: no more sleepovers, not going to assembly, needing you on the phone, refusing the bus, insisting on checking their pulse. Each of those brings relief and teaches the alarm that it was right.

Work the other way, gently and in small steps, and sooner than feels comfortable. The shop with you, then the shop alone. One lesson, then the morning. Praise the going rather than the outcome.

Be careful about your own reassurance becoming part of it, too. Answering the same question for the tenth time, or always being reachable, settles the moment and feeds the pattern.

Look at the conditions

Children in panic are very often short of sleep, running on little food, over-caffeinated from energy drinks, or carrying something they have not mentioned.

Ask about school, friendships, social media, and anything frightening they have seen or heard, in a low-key way and not at bedtime.

And have the obvious physical things checked once: asthma, anemia, thyroid, hearing and vision. One proper check is reassuring; repeated checking is not.

When to get help

Please talk to your child''s doctor if the attacks are happening regularly, if they are missing school, if they have started avoiding ordinary things, or if you are not sure the symptoms are panic.

Say it plainly to the doctor: these look like panic attacks, they are happening weekly, and this is what my child has stopped doing. Panic in children responds very well to treatment, usually in a small number of sessions, and a therapist, school counselor or pediatrician are all reasonable doors in.

Treat it as urgent rather than ordinary if your child is harming themselves, if they talk about not wanting to be here, or if they are frightened of something happening to them that they will not explain. In those cases speak to a doctor the same day rather than working on the panic. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Parenting', 'Panic']::text[], 0, '2026-10-16T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('being-the-one-who-always-reaches-out', 'Being the One Who Always Reaches Out', 'You arrange everything, you message first every time, and you have started wondering what would happen if you stopped. How to read that honestly without writing people off.', 'You booked the table. You picked the date that worked for everyone. You sent the message, as usual, and three of them replied with a thumbs up.

And at some point you did the arithmetic and realized that if you stopped, you might not hear from any of them for a year. That thought is hard to put down once you have had it.

What this actually costs

The practical work is annoying. The meaning you attach to it is what hurts.

Because the question underneath is not about calendars. It is: do these people actually want me, or am I just the one who organizes things? Once that question is live, every unanswered message becomes evidence.

So you keep doing it, slightly resentfully, and the resentment leaks: a sharper tone, a joke about always being the one, a note of keeping score. Which rarely gets you what you want.

Some of it is not about you at all

Before concluding that you are unloved, it is worth knowing how ordinary this is.

Most friendship groups have an organizer, and most of the others are not deciding anything; they simply never initiate, with anybody. They are not keeping a tally. Many of them would be astonished to hear you had been.

People in the thick of small children, shift work, illness or caring for a parent are often not initiating with anyone for years at a stretch. It looks like indifference from outside and it is capacity.

And some people are perfectly happy to be swept along and will cheerfully admit they never organize anything. That is a real limitation in them, not a verdict on you.

Look at what happens when you do reach out

This is the useful test, and it is more informative than who sent the first message.

Do they come? Are they glad to see you? Do they ask about your life and remember the answers? Do they help when something goes wrong?

A friend who never initiates but always turns up and is genuinely pleased is a real friend with one weak skill. A friend who is lukewarm when you do meet, cancels often, or only surfaces when they need something is telling you something different.

Separate those two in your mind, because they call for opposite responses: keep arranging for the first, and spend less on the second.

Say it once, plainly

Most people in this position never actually mention it, and then feel bitter about something nobody was told.

Keep it light and specific rather than an accusation. I love seeing you and I always end up being the one who suggests it. Would you set something up next time?

Said warmly, to someone who cares about you, that usually works at least some of the time. And it is far better than the hinting version, which reads as a mood.

With a group, make it logistical: I am handing the drinks over, somebody else pick the date in November. Groups often reorganize quite happily when the job is simply passed on.

Try a deliberate pause rather than a test

The temptation is to stop and see who notices. That is understandable and it tends to end with you hurt and nobody informed.

A more honest version is a chosen pause: stop initiating with one or two people for a while, not to punish them but to see what you actually want.

Some gaps will fill themselves. Some will not, and the information is worth having. The key is that you are deciding rather than conducting an experiment on people who do not know they are in one.

Then put the effort where it is returned

The hardest part is reallocating, which is not the same as cutting people off.

Keep arranging things with the people who are glad you did. Downgrade quietly where the energy only goes one way: fewer messages, lower expectations, the friendship at the size it actually is rather than the size you keep inflating.

And put some effort into new ground where reciprocity is built in: a class, a team, a choir, a volunteer shift, anything with a fixed time where you are not the one making it happen.

Being the organizer is not a flaw

It is worth saying, because people in this position often decide they are needy.

Someone has to remember the birthdays and book the table, and the people who do it are the reason groups survive at all. It is a kindness and a skill.

What you are allowed to do is want it noticed, ask for some of it back, and stop spending it on people who do not value it. None of that is being difficult.

When to take it further

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if you are regularly certain you are a burden, if one unanswered message can flatten your week, or if you keep ending up in friendships where you do all the work and get little back.

That pattern often has a history behind it: having had to earn attention, or having learned that being useful was how you were kept. It is understandable and it is workable, and most people see a difference within a handful of sessions. And if the loneliness has dropped your mood for weeks rather than days, mention that to a doctor too; it is common, treatable, and much easier to work on with help.', array['Loneliness', 'Self-Esteem']::text[], 0, '2026-10-16T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('going-to-the-gym-when-you-feel-watched', 'Going to the Gym When You Feel Watched', 'The mirrors, the people who clearly know what they are doing, the machine you cannot work out. Why gyms feel exposing, and how to use one anyway.', 'You got as far as the car park twice. The third time you went in, did twelve minutes on the treadmill facing the wall, and left without touching anything else.

It is not the exercise. It is the feeling of being looked at while being a beginner, in a room full of mirrors, in clothes you are not sure about.

Why a gym is an unusually exposing room

Almost everything about the design works against a self-conscious person.

Mirrors on every wall. People who look like they have been there for years. Equipment with no instructions. An etiquette nobody explains, so you are afraid of doing something wrong in public.

Add that you are doing something physical and visible with a body you may not feel good about, and that effort makes you red, sweaty and out of breath in front of strangers.

So the discomfort is not irrational. It is a hard room for a beginner, and knowing that makes it easier to stop treating your reaction as evidence of weakness.

Who is actually looking

Almost nobody, and this is worth hearing properly.

People at a gym are overwhelmingly occupied with their own set, their own phone, their own reflection. The spotlight feeling — the sense that everyone is tracking you — is a normal trick of attention, and it points inward rather than out.

The strong-looking regulars are the least likely to care. They remember being new, they know how the machines work, and the only time most of them look up is to see whether something is free.

And the ones who are glancing are usually waiting for a bench, not forming an opinion.

Go at a quiet hour, on purpose

Make the first weeks easier rather than braver.

Mid-morning, early afternoon, late evening: every gym has dead hours, and staff will tell you which they are if you ask. Avoid the after-work crush for a month.

Pick a smaller or less fashionable place if you can. A council leisure center or a basic gym is often far more comfortable than a shiny one, and tends to have a broader range of ages and shapes in it.

And go with somebody, once or twice, if you have anybody willing. Having a person to talk to removes most of the exposure.

Have a plan on paper

A huge amount of gym anxiety is really not knowing what to do next, which leaves you standing in the middle of the room looking lost.

So write three or four things down, in order, before you go. Ten minutes on a bike, two machines you know, five minutes stretching, done. Thirty minutes total is a full session for a beginner.

Book a free induction, which most gyms include and almost nobody takes. One session with a member of staff showing you how four machines work removes a large part of the problem.

And know that you are allowed to ask. Excuse me, how does this one work, is a completely ordinary sentence in a gym, and the answer is nearly always friendly.

Small practical things that help

Headphones and something familiar to listen to, which puts a wall around you.

Clothes you feel covered in, not what the internet says you should wear. Comfort beats correct.

A water bottle and a towel to hold, which gives your hands something to do between sets.

Facing away from the mirrors if they bother you, which is allowed, and using the corner of the room nobody else wants.

Going in, doing one thing, and leaving. A twelve-minute session that happens is worth more than a perfect hour that does not.

Let it be boring before you let it be hard

The aim for the first month is not fitness. It is for the room to stop being an event.

So go often and briefly, the same time, the same few machines. Repetition is what takes the charge out; intensity does not.

Expect the self-consciousness to fade in a pattern rather than all at once: the entrance gets easy first, then the main floor, then the free weights, then whatever your particular dreaded corner is.

And if a session goes badly, go back within a few days rather than leaving it a month. The gap is what rebuilds the fear.

If it is really about your body

For many people the discomfort is not about competence at all. It is about being seen in a body they have been taught to dislike.

That deserves more honesty than a tip list. You are allowed to be in a gym at any size, with any history, at any level of fitness, and movement is good for you regardless of whether it changes how you look.

If the mirrors are the unbearable part, there are gyms without them, classes in the dark, swimming at quiet hours, and walking, cycling and home workouts that are real exercise rather than a consolation prize.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor before starting if you have a heart or lung condition, are pregnant, have been unwell, or have pain that movement makes worse; and ask a physical therapist rather than guessing if something hurts afterward.

And talk to a doctor or a therapist if feelings about your body are keeping you out of ordinary life, if you are exercising in a way that feels driven rather than chosen, or if the fear of being looked at extends well beyond the gym. Those are common, they respond well to treatment, and a counselor who works with anxiety or with body image will not find any of it silly.', array['Movement', 'Self-Compassion']::text[], 0, '2026-10-16T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-mail-you-have-not-opened', 'The Mail You Have Not Opened', 'A pile of envelopes on the side, some of them official, one of them from the hospital. Why unopened mail gets harder the longer it sits, and how to clear it in one sitting.', 'There are nine envelopes on the shelf by the door. You know roughly what three of them are. One has a window and a logo you do not want to think about. The oldest has been there since July.

Every time you walk past, you feel it. And the longer it sits, the more certain you are that something in there has gone badly wrong.

Why unopened mail gets heavier

Avoidance gives relief now and interest later.

Each day you do not open it, you get a small reprieve, and the pile grows a story: a fine that has doubled, a letter you have missed a deadline on, a test result. Imagination is reliably worse than the contents.

And the pile stops being about letters. It becomes evidence that you cannot manage your own life, which makes opening it feel like confirming something about yourself rather than reading some post.

That is why the most useful move is almost always mechanical: get it open, fast, without thinking about what it means about you.

Do it in one sitting, with a system

Set aside forty minutes and make it slightly pleasant. Tea, radio on, table cleared.

Get three things out: a bin bag, a pen, a piece of paper. Then open everything, in order, without acting on any of it.

Sort into four piles as you go. Nothing needed, which goes straight in the bag and is usually half of it. Needs a reply or a payment. Needs filing. And needs a phone call.

Write each real item on the paper as one line with a date. The paper is the whole point: the pile becomes a list, and a list of six things is manageable where a pile of nine envelopes is not.

Open the frightening one second, not last

People leave the worst envelope until the end, and spend the whole session dreading it.

Open it second. You will have had one easy win, and the rest of the session will be done with the worst already known.

And expect the frightening ones to be smaller than the story. Most official letters say less than you feared, most bills are payable in instalments, and most organizations have a hardship route that is not printed on the front.

Deal with the ones with teeth first

Not everything is equal. A few kinds of letter get worse quickly if ignored, and those come first on the list.

Anything from a court, a tax office, a landlord or lender, a bailiff or collection agency. Anything about benefits or an appeal, which usually has a short deadline. Anything medical, including appointments and results. Anything about a vehicle, insurance or a license.

Subscription renewals, marketing and general reminders can wait a week without consequence.

If money is the problem, say so early

The single most useful thing to know: organizations have processes for people who cannot pay, and they only start when you make contact.

Payment plans, payment holidays, hardship funds, reduced rates and breathing-space arrangements all exist. They are far easier to get before a debt is passed on, and they are routine rather than a favor.

A free debt advice service will also talk to creditors for you, work out what you actually owe, and tell you which letters matter. People often arrive at those services having not opened post for a year, and nobody there is surprised.

Nothing in an envelope requires you to go through it alone.

Then make the next pile smaller

Once it is clear, spend ten minutes making the same thing less likely.

One place for post, opened the day it arrives, standing up, with the bin beside you. Open-and-bin is a thirty-second habit that prevents the whole problem.

Go paperless where you trust yourself to read email, and stay on paper where you do not. Cancel the marketing. Set up direct debits for the predictable bills so fewer letters carry consequences.

And put a fifteen-minute slot in the week for the boring stuff: the same day, the same time. Admin that has an appointment stops accumulating.

When it is more than a pile of letters

If this keeps happening, if opening post makes your chest tight, or if there are rooms in your home you cannot deal with for similar reasons, that is worth taking seriously rather than treating as laziness.

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if avoidance is costing you money, housing, or your health, if you have unopened medical letters, or if the dread around ordinary admin is out of proportion to what is in it. Avoidance of this kind is common, it has nothing to do with intelligence or character, and it responds well to practical help; attention difficulties, anxiety and low mood all produce it, and all three are treatable.

And ask for free money advice rather than carrying a debt alone. A professional adviser will treat an unopened pile as a Tuesday, which is exactly the attitude it needs.', array['Motivation', 'Money']::text[], 0, '2026-10-16T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  title        = excluded.title,
